  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nDry conditions with seasonable temperatures continue today with\nthe western side of the upper level ridging still in place.\nWednesday morning and into the afternoon looks to also remain\ndry however, we will see the advertised trough and associated\ncold front begin to push into the region tomorrow. Some\nuncertainty now exists with latest guidance and synoptic\nconditions indicating that the main energy/cold pool associated\nwith the trough centered over central Canada may push further \nnorth (staying within Canada's borders). This would make the \ncold pool behind the cold front weaker and would also orphan the\nfront and would become washed out and make for much slower\nprogression of the boundary. This boundary though still looks to\nact as a focal point for lifting and will allow for some\nconvection to develop along its lateral. With adequate moisture\npresent (PWATs still around 1.5 to 2 inches) and the slow speed\nof the boundary will make for favorable training of cells to\npotentially create heavier rainfall wherever the boundary sits\nover. WPC has portions of our area under a marginal risk for \nexceeding flash flood guidance each day from Wednesday night\nthrough Friday morning. Looks like the more widespread coverage\nof the marginal flood risk would be on Thursday when the front\nis draped across the CWA. There is currently no expected severe\nweather threat at this time per SPC which makes sense with the\nlack of energy/cold pool and slow movement of the boundary. \n\nThe other big change to the longer term forecast looks to be a\nlonger duration of the cyclonic flow and more of a zonal flow\naloft by the end of the weekend. This would keep us more on the\ncooler and drier side providing continued seasonable conditions\nwith periods of showers, mainly in the afternoon possible. But \nstill need to watch into next week as a building ridge over the\nsoutheast may push northward and that would make for hotter and\nmore humid conditions for the CWA. At this time though the \nlonger range guidance does keep us north of the more \nhumid/hotter airmass. So a bit more uncertainty seems to have \nmade it's way into the forecast this period.",
